1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:22,784
I woke up with Shawna asleep against my chest and Tank arguing with a
nurse about fruit. Nigga, I knew immediately I had survived. Death
might have had trumpets. It did not have my brother explaining that a
pear was now an emotional subject. He can have one when he wakes, the
nurse said. This nigga saved a school. And his stomach needs rest.

2
00:00:23,034 --> 00:00:46,871
His greatness don't. His greatness can wait until breakfast. He got
Blue Thunder, Tank said. He also has a chart. Nigga, you can't put
thunder on a chart. I put his vomiting on it without difficulty. The
room went quiet. Tank shifted in his chair. I kept my eyes closed a
little longer, enjoying the rare sound of my brother losing at full
strength.

3
00:00:47,121 --> 00:01:03,160
I ain't saying give him a buffet, he said. Just have the pear ready.
It is ready. Emotionally ready. The nurse must have looked at him. I
could hear that look from the bed. Would you like to hold it? A pause.
Yes. Nigga took the pear.

4
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:24,550
I knew because the bowl made a little scrape, then his whole voice
became quieter, closer to his chest. Thank you. I had heard Tank
negotiate with dangerous men using fewer words than he'd spent on that
fruit. He'd sounded calmer then, too. My brother wanted a small thing
he could have ready for me. A pear. A laugh.

5
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:47,742
Something he could give with those enormous hands when I opened my
eyes. I let him have another second. Then the nurse said, Please stop
polishing it on your shirt. Nigga, it's a clean shirt. It has been
outside. So has the pear. I washed the pear. And Rosa washed the
shirt. We both brought something to this, ma'am. She laughed despite
herself.

6
00:01:47,992 --> 00:02:12,476
A short sound, immediately recovered. Set it in the bowl. On top? Yes.
Another small clink. The pear had received preferred placement. There,
Tank said. Now when this nigga wakes up, his shit organized. My chest
hurt. Laughing would make it worse. I was going to do it anyway, and
this hospital deserved at least the courtesy of a warning. I opened
one eye.

7
00:02:12,726 --> 00:02:33,788
Nigga, let the woman work. Tank turned so fast his chair scraped
across the floor. His face opened into a grin that told me how
frightened he had been while I wasn't awake to make it easier. There
he is. Shawna lifted her head. For one second she looked lost between
sleep and the last thing she remembered fearing.

8
00:02:34,038 --> 00:02:56,352
Then she saw my eyes open and put her hand against my cheek. Hey. Hey,
baby. She kissed me carefully. That scared me more than a hard kiss
would have. How bad I look? Like somebody hit a very expensive statue
with a truck. Statue still handsome? Obnoxiously. I relaxed. Vega
appeared at the door with Storm behind her.

9
00:02:56,602 --> 00:03:21,654
The doctor checked me before she let anybody explain the outside
world. My ribs were bruised, my shoulder strained, my hands burned,
and my body thoroughly offended by being used as the temporary pathway
for the Heart's charge. Nothing about the new ability had healed the
old wounds. Nothing about the title people were shouting in the
corridors changed the fact that I needed sleep.

10
00:03:21,904 --> 00:03:46,584
No powers today, Vega said. Nigga, I just got them. Then you can enjoy
knowing where they are. Storm laughed softly. The dean looked better.
Tired, but no longer holding the sky up through force of will and a
series of increasingly personal disagreements. She sat beside the bed
and told me the school was stable. Bell and Miriam had severed the old
tower's controls.

11
00:03:46,834 --> 00:04:12,366
The real Heart remained inside the restored central housing. Voss's
armor and the stolen coupling were secured. His surviving guards had
surrendered. Sable and Finn remained in custody, separately, so
neither could improve the other's story before Pike finished asking
questions. The time door had been dismantled completely. Reiss had
watched the final ring come off its mount.

12
00:04:12,616 --> 00:04:30,014
Then he had gone upstairs and slept in a room with an unlocked door. I
listened with Shawna's hand in mine. When Storm finished, I asked
about the people. All sixty-two returnees were safe. The thirty
rescued before the historical mission were safe too.

13
00:04:30,264 --> 00:04:52,686
Dennis from the laundry had gone home with his own keys and had
already returned to work long enough to tell his supervisor which
locked storage rooms he would never enter again. Nobody had required
gratitude as a condition of staying. People were calling families,
arranging rooms, asking questions, refusing questions, resting.

14
00:04:52,936 --> 00:05:18,047
The ones who had come from 1944 were taking the new world at the pace
they could bear. That seemed right to me. Some surprises deserved more
than an afternoon. Storm took my unbandaged wrist gently. You changed
this place. I looked at the window. Beyond it the academy floated in
bright morning air, scarred in places, beautiful in others, still very
much a school.

15
00:05:18,297 --> 00:05:42,282
It needed a little parking correction. She smiled. Yes. Apparently it
did. For two days I was forbidden to leave the medical tower without
somebody sensible beside me. Tank volunteered. Vega looked at him for
so long he withdrew the application himself. Shawna took me walking
instead. Slowly at first. Across the balcony. Down one corridor.

16
00:05:42,532 --> 00:06:02,608
Eventually into the garden where Harlan Moss was repairing the damaged
paths with roots coaxed carefully through the stone. He wore the
polished brown shoes Tank had bought him. That afternoon his wife
arrived. Anne came up the garden steps carrying a small overnight bag
and stopped when she saw him.

17
00:06:02,858 --> 00:06:24,790
She was a little shorter than Shawna, with graying curls and a face
that seemed to lose five months of exhaustion in one breath. Harlan
dropped the pruning shears. Neither of them ran at first. They walked
toward each other, slowly enough to believe each step. Then she
reached him and hit his chest with both hands before folding herself
against it.

18
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:49,366
You still have those shoes, she said through tears. He looked down at
the brown leather. New pair. Same fool wearing them. Your fool. She
cried harder. Tank stood beside me with his mouth pressed into a line.
Rosa slipped her hand into his. He squeezed it without looking away
from the couple. Good shoes, he said quietly. I nodded. Excellent
fucking shoes.

19
00:06:49,616 --> 00:07:13,466
A week later Bell let me sit in the Lamborghini again. He had repaired
every dent except one small mark near the rear fender, which he said
he would preserve if I wanted a reminder of the train. Gold badge
emblems now shone on the doors, and a discreet second strip of police
lights sat above the windshield. Bell said a graduate deserved
appropriate trim.

20
00:07:13,716 --> 00:07:37,637
Nigga, the car had received formalwear. I looked at it. Leave that.
Miriam stood beside him in a clean blue work coat. She had spent the
week learning the academy's engines one careful question at a time.
The floating roads still astonished her. The mechanical problems did
not. You drive as if roads are suggestions, she told me. They been
flexible.

21
00:07:37,887 --> 00:07:59,904
The car has not always appreciated your confidence. Bell nodded so
vigorously all four hands moved. Thank you. She looked at him. But it
is a remarkable car. He nearly cried again. Nigga, I was going to
start carrying tissues for that man. My new ability came with lessons,
which I considered rude but useful.

22
00:08:00,154 --> 00:08:22,138
Storm and Victor helped me learn the difference between feeling charge
and having enough to spend. Bell watched what happened when I passed
it through the car. Vega watched what happened to me afterward and
ended every session before my pride could become an injury. One short
burst could sharpen my reactions and send blue current through my
hands.

23
00:08:22,388 --> 00:08:41,481
A car could carry that charge into a brief surge of acceleration.
Afterward I shook, sweated, and tired quickly. Trying to hold it
longer made everything worse. I could not fly. I could not call up an
endless storm. I could not fix a wound by admiring it.

24
00:08:41,731 --> 00:09:06,593
The body beneath the light still needed food, rest, and a wife willing
to say, Nigga, enough, with final authority. I had that wife. The
power had no chance of making me completely unmanageable. Only
impressively difficult. Two weeks after Voss fell, the academy held
its graduation. They put my name first on the program. Then they put
it in larger print than the title.

25
00:09:06,843 --> 00:09:33,875
Tank unfolded the paper and stared at it. Nigga, this school's
graduating from you. They learned a lot. You attended one class.
Intensively. The ceremony took place in the restored stadium. Every
seat was full. Helicopters and airships hovered beyond the upper rail,
carrying guests who had decided a normal chair lacked ambition. The
orchestra sat beneath a blue canopy. Rosa sang the opening number.

26
00:09:34,125 --> 00:09:53,985
Lena joined her for the second verse, and their voices rose through
the open air without a wall between them. Shawna sat in the front row
wearing the ruby Claudine had given us, set now in a simple pendant
Marcellus made to keep the stone close to her skin. The red flashed
against her blue dress.

27
00:09:54,235 --> 00:10:17,489
She caught me looking and smiled as if the whole stadium existed
mainly to give us more room for that smile. I wore a dark suit, my
gold badge, the cross, and an honors robe so heavy Tank said they had
stitched my ego into the lining. He lifted one corner of it between
two fingers. Nigga, this is upholstery. Academic distinction.

28
00:10:17,739 --> 00:10:41,921
You got a sofa's worth. Porsche came along the row carrying a little
sewing kit. She caught my sleeve, found a loose thread, and clipped it
with the concentration she'd brought to stealing Sable's case. Hold
still. Everybody been telling me that all week. And yet, nigga, here
we are. Victor straightened his own robe. Mine's lighter. Tank looked
him over. You took more classes.

29
00:10:42,171 --> 00:11:05,720
They trusted you with less fabric. That almost makes sense. Don't
encourage the nigga, I said. Porsche stepped back and inspected me.
Turn. I turned. Other way. I turned again. Tank nodded solemnly. Nigga
doing prerequisites. Mercedes came up from the front row to retrieve
Porsche, saw the state of us, and fixed Victor's collar without
asking.

30
00:11:05,970 --> 00:11:28,704
He stood very still, plainly uncertain which institutional rule
governed being fussed over. There, she said. Now you look like the
nice one. I am the nice one. Nigga, let people discover it. He
laughed. I did too. The orchestra tuned behind us, little fragments of
sound finding where they belonged. No alarms. No metal tearing.

31
00:11:28,954 --> 00:11:51,887
Just a hundred people briefly making an ugly noise so they could make
a beautiful one together. Tank brushed the last bit of lint from my
shoulder. You good? Yeah. He pressed his palm there once, firm and
warm. Good, nigga. Then Rosa waved him toward his seat, and he went
without a single argument. Victor watched him go. Does she have
powers?

32
00:11:52,137 --> 00:12:10,957
Nigga, I said, you keep asking that question. Victor stood beside me
in blue. He had his own honors. He had earned them. When the dean
called his name, I stood before anybody else and cheered loud enough
to make him laugh on the way to the platform. That nigga had tried to
beat me.

33
00:12:11,207 --> 00:12:29,503
Then he had helped me save people who would never have known his name
otherwise. I wanted the whole sky to know it now. Petra graduated with
distinction in rescue strength. She accepted her award, turned toward
Mercedes, and kissed her in front of the entire faculty.

34
00:12:29,753 --> 00:12:53,353
Mercedes looked startled for one beautiful second, then put both arms
around her and made the applause last longer. Porsche stood up
cheering. Leon became three men beside her and cheered three times as
loudly. Knox leaned toward Pike. Do all three need separate
identification? Pike closed his eyes. Please let me have one peaceful
afternoon.

35
00:12:53,603 --> 00:13:18,964
Before Storm called my name, the great screen replayed our arrival:
the broken train, my car beneath the platform, Shawna pulling people
through the carriage door. Tank watched himself catch three people at
once and nodded with satisfaction. Nigga, that man strong. That's you,
Rosa said. Exactly. Camera telling the truth. Then Storm said my name.
The stadium stood. All of it.

36
00:13:19,214 --> 00:13:41,548
Nigga, even some people who had been sitting comfortably on air came
down so they could stand properly. I walked onto the platform beneath
the repaired finish arch. The dean held a diploma in one hand and a
gold captain's insignia in the other. Aiden, she said, Top Graduate
Captain of Sky High. The roar rolled across the clouds.

37
00:13:41,798 --> 00:14:09,040
She waited for it to quiet, failed, and continued over it. For
extraordinary command, ingenuity, courage, and devotion to those
placed in his care. Tank shouted, And the driving! And the driving,
Storm agreed. The engineering faculty stood and applauded as one
wounded profession. She gave me the diploma. Then another. I looked at
it. What's this one? An honorary distinction for your vehicle. Nigga.

38
00:14:09,290 --> 00:14:33,362
My car had graduated. I turned toward Tank. He had both hands on his
head. This nigga ride more educated than my cousins! I held up both
diplomas. The stadium lost its fucking mind. Storm stepped aside for
my speech. I looked down at the paper somebody had offered me
backstage. Then folded it and put it in my pocket. Aight, I said.

39
00:14:33,612 --> 00:14:58,065
The room settled into the kind of quiet a man felt in his chest.
First, I want everybody to appreciate that I arrived here late, parked
wrong, destroyed a platform, missed most of my classes, and still
graduated first. Laughter broke across the stands. That's not advice.
Most niggas try that and wind up walking home with a bill. More
laughter. I looked at Shawna.

40
00:14:58,314 --> 00:15:20,019
I came because somebody saw something I did in Vegas and figured I
ought to try it higher up. I brought my people because everywhere I go
gets better when they there. Then I met more people. Some tried to
beat me. Some fed me. Some fixed the car after I made their profession
nervous. Some were singing where nobody could hear them.

41
00:15:20,269 --> 00:15:33,628
Lena looked up from beside Rosa. I hear you now, I said. She pressed
her lips together and nodded. I raised the diploma. This got my name
on it. Fine. Nigga, I like my name.

42
00:15:33,878 --> 00:15:56,240
But every person who held a door, carried a stretcher, told the truth,
brought a shoe, flew back for a friend, or stood beside somebody who
couldn't stand alone—you're in it. I let the silence hold that. Then I
smiled. My wife's still getting the good parking space. The stadium
roared again. Shawna stood, laughing, and lifted both hands toward me.

43
00:15:56,490 --> 00:16:19,296
I came down from the platform before anybody could announce another
honor. Went straight to her. Put my arms around her waist. How I do?
She looked at the diplomas, the badge, the cheering school, the car
waiting beside the field with a blue ribbon tied around its mirror.
Then she looked at me. Damn, boy. I kissed her before she finished.

44
00:16:19,546 --> 00:16:21,935
Nigga, I knew the rest by heart.
